Overall verdict

This set has major internal-governance and operational plausibility problems. The bylaws and charter are unusually engineered around OpenMRS implementation and authority disclaimers, while the meeting minutes conflict directly with the charter’s membership structure and assignment of implementation duties.

Highest-confidence concerns

The current Committee roster does not match the charter

The charter requires one representative each from Medicine, Surgery, Emergency Medicine, Anesthesiology, Pediatrics, Obstetrics and Gynecology, Radiology, and Pathology. The meeting and membership roster instead include representatives for Family Medicine, Pulmonology, Infectious Disease, Nephrology, Endocrinology, Cardiology, Gastroenterology, and two Anesthesiology members. Emergency Medicine, Obstetrics and Gynecology, Radiology, and Pathology are absent. This is a direct structural contradiction.

The minutes assign implementation to the wrong person

The charter and bylaws designate the Senior Ops Analyst in Compliance as the authorized implementer for OpenMRS attributes. The minutes make Alexis Hoyt, Director of Credentialing, responsible for attaching the quarterly-attestation attributes. That conflicts with the repeatedly emphasized exclusive implementation assignment.

The minutes call a special monitoring session a “regular” meeting

The charter requires monthly regular meetings plus quarterly monitoring cycles. The January minutes call the event both a “regular quarterly session” and the next April monitoring cycle the “next regular quarterly session.” That implies the Committee meets quarterly rather than monthly and omits the February and March regular meetings contemplated by the charter.

Incorrect physician registration cycle appears in the bylaws

The bylaws say physicians renew New York registration on a biennial cycle. This repeats the same domain error found throughout the earlier credentialing, HR, training, and provider-record files.

Expired recredentialing does not end or suspend privileges

Section 8.3 says privileges remain in their last granted state if the 36-month cycle expires before governing-body action. This undermines the stated maximum cycle and permits indefinite continuation after recredentialing has lapsed. A mature framework ordinarily requires a defined expiration, deemed resignation, limited extension, or approved contingency process.

A26_Credentials_Committee_Charter.docx
The scope may exceed the parent’s governance authority. The charter applies automatically to every wholly owned subsidiary, including Winston Imaging. Ownership alone does not establish that the parent Board and medical staff govern privileges at every subsidiary facility. The licensing and medical-staff structure would need to support that arrangement.
Temporary and disaster privileges are routed through ordinary Committee review. The charter says the Committee reviews such requests but does not define expedited authority, disaster activation, duration, verification minimums, or retrospective review.
The CMO is the permanent ex officio voting chair. Possible, but it concentrates administrative and recommendation authority and removes meaningful peer selection of the chair.
Named incumbents are hard-coded. Daniel Gould, MD, Alexis Hoyt, Kathleen Hall, and Kevin Caster are embedded in the charter rather than being identified only by office.
The quarterly attestation dates are hard-coded for 2026. A permanent charter would more naturally state the rule without inserting one year’s calendar.
A meeting is required on the 15th even when it falls on a weekend or holiday. Electronic submission deadlines can remain fixed, but requiring a formal Committee meeting on every calendar-day 15th is operationally awkward.
Implementation language overwhelms the governance purpose. Several pages define business-day counting, OpenMRS field names, work-log statuses, one implementer’s duties, and prohibited conduct. Those details belong more naturally in an SOP or systems-control procedure.
The Committee Chair resolves ambiguous Board actions. If the governing-body action is incomplete or ambiguous, the chair of the recommending committee should not unilaterally clarify the Board’s decision. The matter should return to the authorized governance source.
The 10-business-day SLA allows the operational system to disagree with an effective Board action for two weeks. No interim communication or manual control is described.
OpenMRS is again treated as the operational privileging platform. Only two free-text privilege fields are identified, which would be inadequate for facility-specific, procedure-level, date-effective privileges.
All Committee materials are characterized as peer-review materials. Legal protection is not created merely by labeling every packet, minute, or administrative record as peer review.
The charter grants broad file access to the operational implementer. A person entering approved fields would not necessarily need access to full credentials packets or Committee deliberations.
Amendment protection is tied to one job. Section 9.2 prohibits extending implementation authority to anyone other than the named role without concurrent bylaws amendments. That is overly rigid for an administrative systems function.
A13_Medical_Staff_Privileging_Bylaws_Excerpt.docx
The bylaws read like a litigation position rather than medical-staff bylaws. They repeatedly emphasize that only the Board grants privileges, OpenMRS never grants them, the analyst never decides, and statuses cannot be inferred across dimensions.
The excerpt is tailored around one unusual quarterly-attestation control. A significant portion of the privileging bylaws addresses missing attestations, aliases, NPIs, and three named OpenMRS attributes.
Quarterly privilege-scope attestation is atypical. It may be a local control, but the file provides no rationale for requiring every practitioner to repeatedly attest to already granted delineated privileges.
A Type 1 NPI is treated as the definitive identity key. NPI matching can support reconciliation, but it should not by itself determine whether the correct practitioner executed the required attestation.
Registration lapse automatically triggers claims investigation. Reviewing affected services is prudent, but opening a compliance investigation in every case is categorical and may overstate what is often an administrative renewal failure.
An operational hold suspends scheduling and billing while being described as non-restrictive. This is a de facto inability to exercise the privileges even if the formal privilege record remains unchanged.
Restoration mechanics are unclear. After registration is renewed, the document says the privileges remain pending governing-body determination rather than explaining whether exercisability returns automatically or requires a fresh Board action.
The governing-body minutes are the authoritative privilege record. General Board minutes may document approval, but detailed delineations commonly require approved attachments or a credentialing repository. The document does not accommodate that structure.
Restrictions are recorded by overwriting a single scope field. That is weak for maintaining privilege history, conditions, facility scope, procedure-specific restrictions, concurrent grants, and expiration dates.
A termination is recorded by clearing the privilege field. Clearing data destroys the visible current-state explanation and is weaker than recording an explicit terminated status with effective date and linked action.
Thirty-six months runs from the most recent privileging action. A mid-cycle modification or restoration could unintentionally reset the entire recredentialing cycle even if it did not include a full recredentialing review.
Scope includes every site operated by every subsidiary. Again, ownership does not by itself establish a unified medical staff or Board privileging authority.
A14_Credentials_Committee_Meeting_Minutes_Q1_2026.docx
The exception population is omitted. The minutes approve a closed list of unnamed providers, so the official minutes do not establish who was subject to the Committee action.
The cohort total is not stated. The minutes provide the confirmed table and mention a “small number” of exceptions but do not state the complete cohort size or exact exception count.
The Committee reviewed every confirmed provider row by row. For a routine attestation control, this is an inefficient use of physician committee time and feels staged for evidentiary completeness.
The Chair prepared the minutes. The attendee list identifies Alexis Hoyt as Committee Secretary, making it odd that Daniel Gould, MD prepared and executed them on the meeting date.
Minutes were certified before member approval. The charter says draft minutes are approved at the following regular meeting. The chair nevertheless certifies them as a true and correct record on January 15.
The portal outage is documented without an incident reference.
Secure email and paper attestations are accepted without describing identity validation or repository upload controls.
Committee members appear within the attestation cohort they are reviewing. That may be permissible, but the minutes do not address recusals or whether members participated in accepting their own attestations.
All motions carried unanimously. Possible, but combined with the highly scripted language and row-by-row review, it contributes to a synthetic governance-record appearance.
The minutes repeatedly restate legal distinctions instead of documenting deliberation. They read like a formal narrative designed to prove that no privileging action occurred.
Outreach timing conflicts internally. The narrative and motion say outreach should begin within one business day, but the action log makes it due January 20, several days after the January 15 meeting.
Attribute attachment responsibility contradicts the controlling documents. As noted above, Alexis Hoyt receives an implementation task reserved elsewhere to the Senior Ops Analyst in Compliance.
Cross-file contradictions
The charter’s required specialties do not match the actual voting membership.
The bylaws and charter reserve OpenMRS implementation to the Senior Ops Analyst, while the minutes assign it to the Director of Credentialing.
The charter requires monthly meetings, while the minutes describe quarterly sessions as the regular cadence.
The bylaws say quarterly-attestation attributes have no scheduling or billing effect, while other documents described quarterly attestation as affecting those operations.
The charter says Alexis Hoyt coordinates packets and serves as staff resource, while the minutes call her Committee Secretary, a role not assigned in the charter.
The minutes say the control is required by the Credentialing Policy Manual and charter, while the charter says it is required by the bylaws.
The charter’s voting structure conflicts materially with the previously reviewed official Committee Membership Roster.
