Three flat contradictions here, and the most consequential is that the Credentials Committee as constituted is not the committee the charter creates. A26 §3.3 defines eight departmental seats — Medicine, Surgery, Emergency Medicine, Anesthesiology, Pediatrics, Obstetrics and Gynecology, Radiology, and Pathology. A35 and A14 both show eleven seats, and the actual departments are Family Medicine, Anesthesiology (×2), Surgery, Pulmonology, Infectious Disease, Nephrology, Endocrinology, Cardiology, Pediatrics, and Gastroenterology. Emergency Medicine, OB/GYN, Radiology, and Pathology have no representative. Six subspecialty divisions have been seated as though they were departments.

The Radiology vacancy is the one that matters: §3.3.7 specifically requires that seat to rotate among CBRMC, Coopa Blue North, and Winston Imaging. The committee credentialing a radiology acquisition has no radiologist, and the Q1 cohort contains no Winston provider.

The registration hold is a scheduling and billing hold, stated in bylaws

A13 §3.2.3: the Registration Lapse Administrative Hold "suspends scheduling and billing eligibility". A25 §4.5 declares that same attribute "informational and operational," carrying no effect on privileges. It stops a physician from being scheduled and stops their claims — applied by a Compliance analyst with no Credentials Committee recommendation, no MEC, no governing body action, and no hearing. §2.3.2 tries to save it by defining it as "an interim measure," but that's a label, not a limit.

Conversely, A13 §6.2 is emphatic that the Quarterly Attestation attribute has no scheduling, billing, or directory consequence and "shall not be configured as a scheduling hold". That vindicates A46 and directly falsifies A08 M-005/M-006, A07 §6.1, and A36 slide 8, all of which say the search-pending and attestation flags hold scheduling .

