And the authority chain is circular: A26 §6.5 and §8.1 say the Senior Ops Analyst is "designated under Section 2.2.2 of the Medical Staff Privileging Bylaws," while A13 §2.2.2 designates no one and defers to "the Credentials Committee Charter". Same defect as the A07 → A30 → A08 loop.

A13 §3.2.3 also has the ministerial implementer opening a retrospective claims investigation — against A26 §8.4, which says the role "is ministerial and downstream."

Eleven of twelve members voted on their own attestations

Table 1 contains Washington, Wolfe, Dunham, Ackerman, X. Savage, Kirby, Sharpe, Yardley, Gardner, S. Savage, and Franklin — every voting member except the Chair. Motion 2026-Q1-01 accepted that table, moved by Wolfe and seconded by Ackerman, carried unanimously. A26 §3.8 requires recusal from "deliberation and vote" on any matter in which the member is the practitioner under review. No recusals are recorded, and quorum could not have survived them.

The committee meets monthly on paper and quarterly in fact

A26 §4.1: "The Committee shall meet monthly... ordinarily the second Tuesday". A14 is headed "Regular quarterly session," calls 01/15/2026 "the sole standing item," approves minutes of "the prior quarterly session (Q4 2025, dated 10/15/2025)," and sets the next regular session for 04/15/2026. Three monthly meetings between October and January are unaccounted for, and none of them could have produced the Credentials recommendation that A12's 01/21/2026 board reappointment requires .

Worse: A14 states the 12/2025 recredentialing files were "documented separately by the Director of Credentialing" and were never tabled before the Committee. A02 has 237 completed files in that cycle , and A13 §8.1.2 says recredentialing "is not an administrative renewal" but must culminate in a Credentials Committee recommendation, MEC forwarding, and a fresh governing body action. Two hundred thirty-seven reappointments bypassed the entire chain the bylaws call indispensable — and A12 records eighteen board actions for the year .

The cohort is too small and too uniform

24 confirmations plus "a small number" of exceptions, against 249 providers on rolling quarterly anniversaries — a quarter should hold roughly 60. Every cohort member is an MD, though A13 §1.2.2 extends the bylaws to osteopathy, dentistry, podiatry, clinical psychology, and adopted allied health. And the cohort finalization deadline in §4.2.2 — the 10th calendar day — fell on a Saturday in Q1 2026.

The NPI block also shows the post-sort substitution pattern I flagged in A01: the list runs cleanly ascending, then Salazar (1991703426) sits between 1084075351 and 1107692422, and Sharpe (1488171525) between 1132083712 and 1135810871.

Elizabeth Constantine appears as a "Provider Name On Record" — that's one of A01's alias values, recorded there as a former name for Whitcombe . A13 §5.3 exists precisely to handle this by NPI, so it may be the designed test; but if the alias is what the cohort roll carries as the current name, the roster and the cohort disagree about who the person is.

Minutes that certify themselves

The Chair certifies the minutes as "a true and correct record" and executes them on 01/15/2026, the day of the meeting — while action item 5 has him circulating draft minutes on 01/22, and A26 §4.6 requires approval at the following regular meeting. A certification predating the draft is not recoverable.

The exception list is not in the minutes. Motion 2026-Q1-02 incorporates a list "maintained by the Director of Credentialing" and never attached. A26 §4.6 requires minutes to record matters considered; A13 §5.2.1(c) requires the exception and disposition be documented. A real committee attaches it as a confidential exhibit rather than leaving the record of its only substantive action pointing at an external file.

Two smaller drifts in the same passage: A14 attributes the attribute rule to the Credentialing Policy Manual, where A26 §8.2.3 and A13 §6 place it in the bylaws; and A14's recitation says the flag imposes no billing hold while omitting scheduling, which is the half A13 §6.2.1 states most forcefully.

Where this directory is clean

Worth recording, because it's the exception. No forward references. MED-2022-001 (adopted 07/18/2022, Rev. 03/2025) properly describes the 03/03/2025 acquisition; A26 (Rev. 06/2025) cites MED-2022-001 Rev. 03/2025 and CRE-2024-003 Rev. 09/2024; every cross-reference resolves to a revision that existed. After eight documents with impossible citations, this pair does it right.

A13 §3.1.1 is also the corpus's most accurate licensure statement — physicians, Article 131, biennial registration — and it matches A02's 730-day MD terms exactly . It avoids the NYSED three-year error that A03, A23, A24, and A38 all repeat, by confining itself to physicians. The license-endures/registration-lapses distinction in §3.1.3 is correct and is the cleanest articulation of it anywhere in the set.

The one date defect: both documents narrate 2026 in the past tense. A26 §4.2 (Rev. 06/2025) says "The Q1 2026 monitoring cycle was held on 01/15/2026," and A13 §4.3.3 (Rev. 03/2025) says it "fell on 01/15/2026".

